## Why This Is Asked

Tests for real-time collaboration systems must handle offline edits, eventual consistency, and deterministic merges. This question evaluates your ability to architect an end-to-end flow that exercises offline queues, CRDT-based merging, and version history while ensuring test isolation and reproducibility.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first collaboration with optimistic UI updates
- CRDT-based merge algorithms ensuring deterministic outcomes
- Test data isolation using per-test seeds and unique identifiers
- Server-sync mocks with controlled timing for deterministic test results

## Code Example

```javascript
// Setup two Playwright contexts for concurrent user simulation
const contextA = await browser.newContext();
const contextB = await browser.newContext();
const pageA = await contextA.newPage();
const pageB = await contextB.newPage();

// Seed a shared base document
const docId = await seedDocument({ content: 'Initial paragraph.' });

// Simulate offline for both users
await setNetworkOffline(pageA);
await setNetworkOffline(pageB);

// User A edits the paragraph
await pageA.goto(`/doc/${docId}`);
await pageA.locator('.editor').fill('User A edited this.');

// User B edits the same paragraph offline
await pageB.goto(`/doc/${docId}`);
await pageB.locator('.editor').fill('User B edited that.');

// Reconnect both users to trigger CRDT sync
await setNetworkOnline(pageA);
await setNetworkOnline(pageB);

// Wait for sync completion
await expect(pageA.locator('.editor')).toHaveText('Expected merged content');
await expect(pageB.locator('.editor')).toHaveText('Expected merged content');

// Verify idempotent replay
const versionBefore = await getDocumentVersion(docId);
await triggerSyncAgain(pageA);
const versionAfter = await getDocumentVersion(docId);
expect(versionAfter).toBe(versionBefore);

// Verify version history
const history = await getVersionHistory(docId);
expect(history).toContain('User A edited this.');
expect(history).toContain('User B edited that.');
```

## Why This Is Asked
Offline-first reliability with service workers is a critical requirement in modern e-commerce applications. This question evaluates whether a candidate understands how to test caching behavior, connectivity state changes, and retry logic in end-to-end tests.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX patterns with service workers
- Network throttling and failure simulation (Cypress or Playwright network interception)
- Test data isolation with fixtures or unique identifiers
- Idempotent order placement to prevent duplicate charges

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Intercept network requests to simulate offline behavior
cy.intercept('POST', '/api/payment', (req) => {
  req.reply({ statusCode: 503, body: { error: 'Network error' } });
}).as('paymentRequest');

// Simulate going offline
cy.networkIdle(); // or use Cypress offline plugin
cy.window().then((win) => {
  win.navigator.onLine = false;
});

// Verify offline UI and localStorage persistence
cy.get('[data-testid=offline-banner]').should('be.visible');
cy.getLocalStorage('cart').should('exist');

// Restore network and retry
cy.networkOn();
cy.wait('@paymentRequest').then(() => {
  cy.get('[data-testid=order-confirmation]').should('be.visible');
});

// Verify idempotent order creation
cy.get('@paymentRequest').its('response.statusCode').should('eq', 200);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests data isolation, asynchronous ETL, eventual consistency, and idempotency in a real-world multi-tenant dashboard.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation
- Mocking external data sources
- Idempotent operations
- Eventual consistency and latency
- Regional replication and failover

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright-like sketch
test('dataset ETL end-to-end with tenant isolation', async ({page}) => {
  const tenant = await createTenant();
  const dsId = `ds-${Date.now()}`;
  await loginAsTenantUser(tenant);
  await page.goto('/datasets');
  await page.fill('#name', dsId);
  await page.click('#create');
  // mock external sources and trigger ETL via API
  await mockExternalDataForTenant(tenant, dsId);
  await triggerETL(dsId);
  await waitForDashboardData(tenant, dsId);
  // idempotency check
  await page.click('#create', {force:true});
  await waitForDashboardData(tenant, dsId);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate data correctness if ETL shows partial results due to a region outage?
- What metrics would you collect to monitor test determinism across runs?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical E2E skills: gating UX with feature flags, deterministic tests, data seeding, and test isolation using Cypress or Playwright.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and gating
- Test data seeding per run
- LocalStorage state as idempotence marker
- Network/mocks to deterministically simulate flag responses
- Time-bounded execution for beginner scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright skeleton
test('onboarding respects flag', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/flags**', r => r.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ onboarding: true }) }));
  await page.goto('https://app.local/');
  await expect(page.locator('#onboard-modal')).toBeVisible();
  await page.locator('#finish').click();
  const stored = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('onboarding_complete'));
  expect(stored).toBe('true');
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('#onboard-modal')).toBeHidden();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to multi-tenant setups with per-tenant flag overrides?
- How would you ensure the test remains reliable when the flag toggles during a session?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design end-to-end tests for complex, privacy-conscious configurations spanning multiple services, data residency requirements, and dynamic rollout strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Feature-flag propagation across 120 microservices via event bus
- Canary vs. percentage-based rollouts and race condition handling
- Per-tenant caches with data residency enforcement
- Cache invalidation and immutable audit logging
- Fault injection: service outages, retry logic, and exponential backoff

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test skeleton (using your preferred E2E framework)
async function testFlagRollout() {
  const tenants = await seedTenants([
    { id: 'tenant-1', region: 'us-east' },
    { id: 'tenant-2', region: 'eu-west' },
    { id: 'tenant-3', region: 'ap-south' }
  ]);

  // Test canary rollout (5%)
  await flagService.update({ key: 'new-feature', rollout: 5 });
  await waitForPropagation(60000);

  // Verify canary distribution
  for (const tenant of tenants) {
    const flag = await getFlag(tenant.id, 'new-feature');
    expect(flag.enabled).toBe(tenant.region === 'us-east');
    await verifyAuditLog(tenant.id, 'new-feature', 'canary');
  }

  // Test 50% rollout
  await flagService.update({ key: 'new-feature', rollout: 50 });
  await waitForPropagation(60000);

  // Verify all services observe flag
  const serviceFlags = await collectFromAllServices('new-feature');
  expect(serviceFlags.every(f => f.enabled)).toBe(true);

  // Verify cache invalidation
  for (const tenant of tenants) {
    const cacheStatus = await getCacheStatus(tenant.id);
    expect(cacheStatus.invalidated).toBe(true);
  }
}

// Fault injection: service outage
async function testFlagServiceOutage() {
  await simulateServiceOutage('flag-service');
  await flagService.update({ key: 'fallback-test', rollout: 100 });

  // Verify retries and eventual consistency
  await waitForPropagation(120000);
  const flags = await collectFromAllServices('fallback-test');
  expect(flags.every(f => f.enabled)).toBe(true);
}

// Load test: rapid toggles and race conditions
async function testRapidToggles() {
  const toggles = Array(100).fill().map((_, i) => ({
    key: `feature-${i}`,
    rollout: Math.random() * 100
  }));

  await Promise.all(toggles.map(t => flagService.update(t)));
  await waitForPropagation(60000);

  // Verify consistency under load
  for (const toggle of toggles) {
    const flags = await collectFromAllServices(toggle.key);
    const enabledCount = flags.filter(f => f.enabled).length;
    const expectedCount = Math.floor(120 * (toggle.rollout / 100));
    expect(Math.abs(enabledCount - expectedCount)).toBeLessThan(5);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes proficiency with real-time systems in E2E tests, including multi-user concurrency, latency handling, offline scenarios, and data isolation. It reveals how you design deterministic, reproducible tests for complex client-server interactions.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time collaboration testing
- CRDT/OT reconciliation validation
- Multiple browser contexts orchestration
- Latency injection and offline/reconnect paths
- Data isolation and deterministic ordering
- Test data management and cleanup

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright scaffold (pseudo)
const docId = `test_${Date.now()}`
const context1 = await browser.newContext()
const page1 = await context1.newPage()
const context2 = await browser.newContext()
const page2 = await context2.newPage()

// Setup: both users open same document
await page1.goto(`/editor/${docId}`)
await page2.goto(`/editor/${docId}`)

// Concurrent edits with latency injection
await page1.route('**/socket.io/**', route => {
  setTimeout(() => route.continue(), 100) // Inject 100ms latency
})

await page1.type('[contenteditable]', 'User 1 text')
await page2.type('[contenteditable]', 'User 2 text')

// Verify real-time updates and CRDT reconciliation
const finalContent1 = await page1.textContent('[contenteditable]')
const finalContent2 = await page2.textContent('[contenteditable]')
expect(finalContent1).toBe(finalContent2) // Deterministic final state
```

